From: Alejandro Hernandez <alejandro@enedino.org>
Subject: [PATCH] bmake: skip racy cmd-interrupt test on Linux

The cmd-interrupt test's compat case sends SIGINT to make and then sleeps
before creating its target. It expects make to leave that target behind,
recording a known failure as "expected-fail". The result depends on signal
delivery and process scheduling; Linux CI can instead report
"unexpected-ok" and abort the bootstrap of an otherwise usable bmake.

Use the existing BROKEN_TESTS mechanism to exclude this test on Linux,
as is already done for other hosts. Keep all other bootstrap tests and
their failure handling enabled. This affects the host-side bootstrap,
regardless of the FreeBSD target architecture.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Linux bootstrap test workaround]

--- a/contrib/bmake/unit-tests/Makefile
+++ b/contrib/bmake/unit-tests/Makefile
@@ -509,6 +509,11 @@
 BROKEN_TESTS+= shell-ksh
 .endif
 
+.if ${.MAKE.OS} == "Linux"
+# The compat interrupt case races signal handling against target creation.
+BROKEN_TESTS+= cmd-interrupt
+.endif
+
 .if ${.MAKE.OS:NIRIX*} == ""
 BROKEN_TESTS+= \
 	cmd-interrupt \
